[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Data Structures in Problem Solving: Hash Tables, Heaps, and DSU"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Efficient problem solving in computer science often relies on choosing the right data structures. This tutorial explores three foundational advanced data structures: Hash Tables, Heaps, and Disjoint Set Unions (DSU), analyzing their operational mechanics and practical applications in competitive programming."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Hash Tables: O(1) Average Time Complexity Lookups"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hash tables map keys to values using a hash function. They provide average O(1) time complexity for insertions, deletions, and searches, making them essential for frequency counting, caching, and detecting duplicates."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Collision Resolution: Handled primarily through chaining (linked lists at each bucket) or open addressing (linear/quadratic probing)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Load Factor: The ratio of elements to buckets; high load factors trigger resizing and rehashing."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"# Example: Two Sum problem using a Hash Map in Python
def two_sum(nums, target):
    seen = {}
    for i, num in enumerate(nums):
        complement = target - num
        if complement in seen:
            return [seen[complement], i]
        seen[num] = i
    return []"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Heaps (Priority Queues): Dynamic Extremum Retrieval"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A heap is a specialized tree-based data structure satisfying the heap property: in a max-heap, for any given node C, the value of C is less than or equal to its parent. In a min-heap, it is greater than or equal. Heaps allow O(log N) insertions and deletions, and O(1) access to the maximum or minimum element."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Binary Heap Implementation: Typically represented as a dynamic array where for a node at index i, its left child is at 2*i + 1 and right child is at 2*i + 2."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Common Applications: Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm, Prim's minimum spanning tree, and finding the K-th largest element."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"# Example: Finding the Kth largest element using a min-heap
import heapq

def find_kth_largest(nums, k):
    min_heap = []
    for num in nums:
        heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)
        if len(min_heap) > k:
            heapq.heappop(min_heap)
    return min_heap[0]"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Disjoint Set Union (DSU / Union-Find)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Disjoint Set Union is a data structure that tracks a set of elements partitioned into a number of disjoint (non-overlapping) subsets. It supports two primary operations: Find (determine which subset a particular element is in) and Union (join two subsets into a single subset)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Path Compression: Flattens the structure of the tree whenever Find is used, making subsequent operations much faster."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Union by Rank/Size: Always attaches the smaller tree under the root of the higher tree to keep the tree shallow."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example: DSU implementation with Path Compression and Union by Rank in C++
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class DSU {
    vector<int> parent, rank;
public:
    DSU(int n) {
        parent.resize(n);
        rank.resize(n, 0);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    int find(int i) {
        if (parent[i] == i)
            return i;
        return parent[i] = find(parent[i]); // Path compression
    }

    void unite(int i, int j) {
        int root_i = find(i);
        int root_j = find(j);
        if (root_i != root_j) {
            if (rank[root_i] < rank[root_j])
                parent[root_i] = root_j;
            else if (rank[root_i] > rank[root_j])
                parent[root_j] = root_i;
            else {
                parent[root_j] = root_i;
                rank[root_i]++;
            }
        }
    }
};"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Complexity Comparison"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering Hash Tables, Heaps, and DSU provides a robust foundation for tackling complex algorithmic challenges involving searching, streaming data, and graph connectivity."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hash Table: Search O(1) avg, Insert O(1) avg, Delete O(1) avg."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heap: Find-Min/Max O(1), Insert O(log N), Extract-Min/Max O(log N)."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DSU: Find O(alpha(N)), Union O(alpha(N)) where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function."}]
